WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.322
Coding Exercise 5 combines an UPDATE with transaction control.

00:00:04.322 --> 00:00:08.987
You need to make a temporary age change, inspect it, roll it back,

00:00:08.987 --> 00:00:12.477
and then verify that the original value is restored.

00:00:12.477 --> 00:00:16.762
Think of the task as a sequence rather than one long statement.

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:21.932
Start the transaction, make the targeted update, inspect the changed row, undo the transaction,

00:00:21.932 --> 00:00:25.977
and finally select the row again.

00:00:25.977 --> 00:00:29.527
Use Run and Check after the sequence is complete.

00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:32.237
The final verification is important.

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:36.842
A successful rollback should leave the stored age at its original value.

00:00:36.842 --> 00:00:43.422
If the final value remains changed, review whether the rollback was executed inside the same transaction

00:00:43.422 --> 00:00:49.517
before the verification query. For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:49.517 --> 00:00:54.582
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:54.582 --> 00:00:58.137
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:58.137 --> 00:01:02.277
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:09.342
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:09.342 --> 00:01:13.512
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:13.512 --> 00:01:18.219
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
